title: Checking and reindexing a repository
tags: Storage
kind: guide
summary: mf repo check tells whether a repository's store is consistent; mf repo reindex rebuilds its indexes, which repairs most of what check finds.

```
mf -n music repo check       # prints "ok", or one problem per line and exits 1
mf -n music repo reindex     # rebuilds the indexes; prints "reindexed"
```

!! What they look at

A repository's store holds two kinds of data ([[Storage]]):

* the ''primary'' data — the metarecords, their field rows, the operation log. It
  is what you wrote, the source of truth;
* the ''indexes'' (the code calls them the derived key spaces: ids, sets,
  partitions, the forest, trigrams, postings) — computed from the primary data,
  and used to answer queries quickly.

Every write updates both in one transaction, so they should always agree. If they
ever do not (a daemon bug, a crash the store did not survive cleanly), queries give
''wrong answers'' — a metarecord a query misses, a path that resolves to the wrong
thing — but nothing is lost: the primary data is intact.

!! When to use them

Run `check` when queries answer something the data contradicts, after a crash, or
before trusting a store you restored by hand. It changes nothing.

Run `reindex` when `check` reports an index problem. You do not need it after an
upgrade: a store whose indexes are of an older format is reindexed automatically
when it is opened ([[Persisted format changes]]).

!! What check reports, and the repair

`check` prints `ok` on a healthy store. Otherwise it prints one line per problem (a
hundred at most), a summary on stderr, and exits 1. A problem is one of:

|!Kind |!Example line |!Repair |
|an index disagrees with the data |`ids: map 1203 metarecords, the store holds 1204` |`mf repo reindex` |
|a metarecord has several positions in one forest |`metarecord <uuid> holds 2 positions in the 'mfr_path' forest (one per forest): set the field to the one to keep` |set the field to a single value yourself |

The second kind is not damage: an older daemon allowed it, the current one gives it
no meaning ([[One position per forest]]). It lives in the primary data, so
`reindex` cannot choose for you which position to keep — after it, `check` still
reports it.

`reindex` discards the indexes and computes them again from the primary data. It
never loses anything, writes nothing to the operation log, and leaves nothing to
undo. Run `check` again afterwards: it should print `ok` (or only the
several-positions lines).

!! Cost

Both read the whole store, and both hold the repository while they run: queries,
writes and the watcher's flushes on it wait until they finish (other repositories
are unaffected). On a large repository this takes a while.

!! How the check stays within memory

The comparison holds neither side in memory: the indexes of a large repository —
every trigram of every text, per holder — can be larger than the machine's memory,
and the same check runs inside the daemon on every backup, automatic ones included
([[Backups and restore]]). Both sides are streamed into fingerprints (a sum of entry
hashes per index, cut in buckets), and only the buckets whose fingerprints differ
are read a second time, to name the entries that differ. What stays resident is a
parent per forest node. An earlier check that collected everything it compared was
killed for lack of memory on a 3 GB store, taking the daemon with it at every start.
